WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.227
Issuers can switch on powers over the tokens they issue.

2
00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:08.574
The powers are public on the ledger, but almost no wallet shows them.

3
00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:11.345
They decide whether a token is really yours.

4
00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:18.406
An issuer can freeze one holder's trust line, so that holder can only send the token back.

5
00:00:18.406 --> 00:00:21.920
Or it can freeze every holder at once with a global freeze.

6
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:26.679
A deep freeze goes further: a frozen holder can neither send nor receive.

7
00:00:27.129 --> 00:00:31.058
An issuer can also give freezing up for good by setting No Freeze.

8
00:00:31.058 --> 00:00:33.081
It can never be switched back off.

9
00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:38.502
Require Auth means you need the issuer's approval before you can hold the token at all.

10
00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:42.455
Clawback lets the issuer take tokens back out of your account.

11
00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:48.104
It can only be switched on before the issuer has any trust lines, so for that token it is permanent.

12
00:00:48.554 --> 00:00:53.845
And a transfer fee charges a percentage every time holders send the token to each other.

13
00:00:55.545 --> 00:00:58.300
The powers matter less than who can use them.

14
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.566
An account can be signed for by its master key, by a regular key, or by a signer list.

15
00:01:04.016 --> 00:01:07.502
A signer list gives each person a weight and sets a quorum.

16
00:01:07.502 --> 00:01:12.997
With weights three, two, two, one and one, and a quorum of five, just two signers are enough.

17
00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:14.533
Count weights, not people.

18
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:21.249
And if the master key is still enabled, one person holding it can bypass the signer list completely.

19
00:01:21.249 --> 00:01:23.943
NOSHASHI flags this as a master-key bypass.

20
00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:28.506
An issuer whose keys are all disabled or unusable is blackholed.

21
00:01:28.506 --> 00:01:34.676
Nobody can ever sign for it again, so its settings are frozen in time, for better and for worse.

22
00:01:36.376 --> 00:01:43.096
Here are three real certificates, read from validated mainnet on the twenty-fourth of September twenty twenty-six.

23
00:01:43.546 --> 00:01:45.927
Bitstamp and GateHub both score no go.

24
00:01:45.927 --> 00:01:51.439
In each case a single regular key can sign for the issuer, which fails a blocking check.

25
00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:53.568
That is not an accusation.

26
00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:58.474
Keeping freeze rights is normal, and often required, for a regulated issuer.

27
00:01:58.474 --> 00:02:04.284
The certificate simply states what the issuer can still do, so a holder decides knowingly.

28
00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:07.759
Sologenic has set No Freeze and is blackholed.

29
00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:16.702
It scores hold, not go, only because it charges a small transfer fee, which is a warning, and its supply concentration was not measured.

30
00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:20.813
The Authority screen runs six checks.

31
00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:27.851
Two are blocking: the token must not be globally frozen right now, and no single key may control the issuer.

32
00:02:27.851 --> 00:02:30.327
Fail either, and the verdict is no go.

33
00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:38.372
The other four are warnings: freeze not surrendered, permission needed to hold, a transfer fee, and concentrated supply.

34
00:02:38.372 --> 00:02:40.271
Any failed warning gives hold.

35
00:02:40.721 --> 00:02:47.825
You can run the same six checks on any issuer for free, without an account, on the certificate page at noshashi dot app.
